    "conjugate": {
      "word": "CONJUGATE",
      "definitions": [
        {
          "text": "United in pairs; yoked together; coupled.",
          "partOfSpeech": "adjective"
        },
        {
          "text": "(Bot.)",
          "partOfSpeech": "adjective"
        },
        {
          "text": "In single pairs; coupled.",
          "partOfSpeech": "adjective"
        },
        {
          "text": "(Chem.)",
          "partOfSpeech": "adjective"
        },
        {
          "text": "Containing two or more radicals supposed to act the part of a single one. [R.]",
          "partOfSpeech": "adjective"
        },
        {
          "text": "(Gram.)",
          "partOfSpeech": "adjective"
        },
        {
          "text": "Agreeing in derivation and radical signification; -- said of words.",
          "partOfSpeech": "adjective"
        },
        {
          "text": "(Math.)",
          "partOfSpeech": "adjective"
        },
        {
          "text": "Presenting themselves simultaneously and having reciprocal properties; -- frequently used in pure and applied mathematics with reference to two quantities, points, lines, axes, curves, etc. Conjugate axis of a hyperbola (Math.), the line through the center of the curve, perpendicular to the line through the two foci. or hyperbola such that each bisects all chords drawn parallel to the other. the focus of one are received at the focus of the other, especially two concave mirrors so placed that rays proceeding from the principal focus of one and reflected in a parallel beam are received upon the other and brought to the principal focus. whose vertices is the pole of the opposite side with reference to a conic.",
          "partOfSpeech": "adjective"
        },
        {
          "text": "A word agreeing in derivation with another word, and therefore generally resembling it in signification. We have learned, in logic, that conjugates are sometimes in name only, and not in deed. Abp. Bramhall.",
          "partOfSpeech": "noun"
        },
        {
          "text": "(Chem.)",
          "partOfSpeech": "noun"
        },
        {
          "text": "A complex radical supposed to act the part of a single radical. [R.]",
          "partOfSpeech": "noun"
        },
        {
          "text": "To unite in marriage; to join. [Obs.] Sir H. Wotton.",
          "partOfSpeech": "verb"
        },
        {
          "text": "(Gram.)",
          "partOfSpeech": "verb"
        },
        {
          "text": "To inflect (a verb), or give in order the forms which it assumed in its several voices, moods, tenses, numbers, and persons.",
          "partOfSpeech": "verb"
        },
        {
          "text": "To unite in a kind of sexual union, as two or more cells or individuals among the more simple plants and animals.",
          "partOfSpeech": "verb"
        }
      ],
